{0 Caching server functions}

[[@cache]] on a top-level function that returns a promise keeps its result, so
equal calls run the function once. It works in server code: loaders, server
components, metadata and server functions. The attribute comes from
[server-reason-react.ppx], and the runtime is {!React.Cache}.

{[
[@cache]
let viewer = (~id: int): Lwt.t(User.t) => Users.fetch(id);
]}

Every argument needs a type annotation. The ppx builds the cache key from the
arguments, so two calls with equal arguments share one entry.

{1:scopes Scopes}

[scope] decides how long an entry lives.

{ul
  {li [Request], the default. One entry per key for one request. The router
      opens the request scope in [RouterServer.serve] and
      [RouterServer.serveFormAction] and keeps it for the whole streamed
      body, so a loader and a server component that call [viewer(~id=1)] run
      it once. [ReactServerDOM.render_html], [render_model_value] and
      [ReactDOM.renderToStream] join an open scope, and open their own when
      none is open. Outside every scope, each call runs the function.}
  {li [Process]. Entries live in this process across requests, until [ttl]
      runs out, [max] is full, or {!React.Cache.expire} drops them.}
}

{[
[@cache {scope: Process, ttl: 30., tags: ["user:" ++ string_of_int(id)] }]
let appearance = (~id: int): Lwt.t(Appearance.t) => Users.appearance(id);
]}

A [Process] entry is one value for every request. Do not read the request,
the user or cookies inside the function. The function runs outside the
request scope that started it, so [Request] caches that it calls do not
share entries with that request. A [Process] cache is local to one process:
two servers keep two copies, and each one keeps its copy until [ttl] runs out
or the server calls [expire] itself.

{1:fields Fields}

{table
  {tr {th Field} {th Type} {th Default} {th Meaning}}
  {tr {td [scope]} {td [Request] or [Process]} {td [Request]} {td How long an entry lives.}}
  {tr {td [ttl]} {td [float], seconds} {td none} {td [Process] only. An entry
      older than [ttl], counted from when its promise resolved, runs the
      function again.}}
  {tr {td [max]} {td [int]} {td [1000]} {td [Process] only. When the cache is
      full, the least recently used entry goes.}}
  {tr {td [errors]} {td [Keep] or [Retry]} {td [Keep] for [Request], [Retry]
      for [Process]} {td [Keep] shares a failed promise with later calls, as
      React's [cache] does. [Retry] drops it, so the next call runs again.}}
  {tr {td [tags]} {td [list(string)], can use the arguments} {td [[]]}
      {td Names for {!React.Cache.expire}.}}
  {tr {td [key]} {td [string], can use the arguments} {td every argument}
      {td The inputs that make two calls equal. Use it to leave out an
      argument that does not change the result.}}
}

The ppx rejects [ttl] and [max] with [scope: Request], an unknown field,
[let rec], a local binding, and an argument without a type annotation.

{1:keys Keys}

The ppx picks a key encoder from each argument's type:

{ul
  {li [int], [string], [bool], [float], [int64], [char] and [unit], and
      [option], [list], [array] and tuples of them: {!React.Cache.Key}.}
  {li Any other type [t]: its RSC codec, [[%to_rsc: t]], read by
      [RSC.to_cache_key]. Derive it with [[@deriving rsc]]. A type whose RSC
      codec round-trips gets keys that never collide.}
}

[()] and [_] arguments are not part of the key. Each encoder is injective,
and the key joins the arguments with their lengths, so [("a", "bc")] and
[("ab", "c")] give different keys.

{1:expire Expire}

{[
let%lwt () = Users.setAppearance(id, value);
React.Cache.expire(~tag="user:" ++ string_of_int(id));
]}

[expire] drops every [Process] entry with the tag, and every entry with the
tag in the current request. A call that is still running finishes. The next
call for its key runs the function again.

{1:browser In the browser}

On Melange, [[@cache]] with [scope: Request] leaves the plain function, and
[scope: Process] is a compile error, because a browser has no process cache.

{1:without Without the attribute}

The attribute writes these calls:

{[
let viewer = {
  let cache = React.Cache.make(~name="Users.viewer", ~scope=Request, ());
  (~id: int) =>
    React.Cache.run(cache, ~key=React.Cache.Key.(parts([int(id)])), () =>
      Users.fetch(id)
    );
};
]}

[React.cache] and [RouterServer.memo] use the same request scope.
